# Accepted vulnerability risk. Read by the Trivy scans in .github/workflows/ci.yml
# and .github/workflows/image-scan.yml; the contract below is enforced by
# tests/test_trivyignore.py, not by Trivy, which accepts an entry with no expiry
# and then suppresses it forever.
#
# One finding per line, immediately preceded by a comment giving the reason:
#
#   # ubuntu has no fixed package yet; revisit at the next base-image bump
#   CVE-2026-12345 exp:2026-12-01
#
# The expiry is mandatory and must be yyyy-mm-dd. Past it, Trivy stops
# suppressing and the finding blocks the gate again — which is the point: an
# acceptance with no end date is indistinguishable from a forgotten one.
#
# Suppression is per-ID and nothing else is an accepted-risk mechanism. Lowering
# the scans' `severity`, dropping the gate step, or setting its `exit-code` to 0
# silences every finding at once and is not the same decision as accepting one.
#
# Per-ID means the ID is matched ANYWHERE in the image, not scoped to the target
# it was found in — the plain format has no `paths:` field. Before adding an entry,
# check no other target carries the same ID where it would be fixable: hiding a
# bumpable finding is the failure mode this cannot express.
#
# If the gate goes red on an ID listed below, suspect a lapsed expiry first. Trivy
# stops suppressing silently on that date and reports the finding as if it were
# new, with nothing pointing back at this file.

# gh vendors golang.org/x/mod v0.39.0 and the fix needs 0.40.0, which shipped
# 2026-08-13, three weeks before gh 2.99.0 — upstream skipped it once already.
# The version is compiled into the binary, so there is nothing to bump here;
# only a new gh release can clear it. At expiry, check whether gh has picked
# up x/mod 0.40.0 and drop this if so.
CVE-2026-56864 exp:2026-11-04
# Same upstream gap, same binary: gh's vendored golang.org/x/mod v0.39.0
# against a fix that needs 0.40.0. Each entry carries its own reason because
# suppression is per-ID and the two are only incidentally related.
CVE-2026-56865 exp:2026-11-04
# gh 2.102.0 (apt, floats to the latest release) is built with Go 1.27.1; the
# net/http fix needs Go 1.26.9 or 1.27.2, both tagged 2026-10-08, after that
# release. The Go toolchain pin carries the fix, so gh is the only carrier left
# and this hides nothing a bump here can clear. The expiry is about one gh
# release cycle out: at expiry, check that the image's gh is built with
# Go >= 1.27.2 and drop this.
CVE-2026-78667 exp:2026-10-23
# Same gh binary and Go 1.27.1 gap: crypto/tls ECH extensions, same fix
# versions. Its own entry because suppression is per-ID.
CVE-2026-97031 exp:2026-10-23
# Same gh binary and Go 1.27.1 gap: HTTP/2 SETTINGS flood DoS in net/http and
# golang.org/x/net (fixed in Go 1.27.2 / x/net 0.60.0). Trivy's DB picked it up
# after 2026-10-09; gh 2.102.0 is still the latest release. Same expiry.
CVE-2026-78669 exp:2026-10-23
# npm (pins/npm.env) vendors brace-expansion 5.0.9; the fix needs 5.0.10,
# released upstream 2026-09-14. No npm release bundles it yet: 11.21.0 and
# 12.2.0 (2026-09-30) still ship 5.0.9. Only npm's own tree carries it. npm is
# now an automated pin, so the weekly refresh bumps it once a release carrying
# the fix has soaked (7 days). npm releases every 1-4 weeks, so 2026-11-04
# allows two release cycles plus the soak. At expiry, check the bundled version
# in the pinned npm and drop this if it's fixed.
CVE-2026-102276 exp:2026-11-04
# Same package and gap as above; the fix needs brace-expansion 5.0.11.
CVE-2026-102278 exp:2026-11-04
# npm vendors undici 6.28.0; the fix needs 6.28.1, released upstream
# 2026-09-04 and not yet bundled by 11.21.0 or 12.2.0. Same expiry reasoning.
CVE-2026-19534 exp:2026-11-04
# Scope note: trivy ignores are per-CVE-ID and image-wide, unlike the path- and
# version-scoped .grype.yaml rule for the same two CVEs. That is fine while
# /opt/az holds the image's only cryptography 48.x copy (pins/az-requirements.txt);
# re-check if another component ever vendors cryptography.
# az (/opt/az): azure-cli-core 2.90.0 pins msal==1.36.0, which caps
# cryptography <49, so the venv resolves 48.0.1; the fix is 50.0.0. Full
# azure-cli ships the same pin — only an azure-cli-core release that moves msal
# can clear it (msal 1.39.0 already allows <51). PKCS#7 EnvelopedData
# decryption, which the Azure DevOps commands never call. At expiry, check
# whether pins/az.env's core release has moved msal and drop this if so.
CVE-2026-69247 exp:2026-11-04
# Same msal==1.36.0 cap on the same cryptography 48.0.1 in /opt/az (fix 49.0.0):
# X.509 path building on duplicate self-signed intermediates. Its own entry
# because suppression is per-ID.
CVE-2026-69249 exp:2026-11-04
